      "content": "# Tensor Movement in a Four-Node Ring Network for Distributed Neural Network Inference\n\nDistributed neural network inference splits a large model across multiple devices when a single node cannot hold all weights or meet latency targets. A four-node ring topology (N0 \u2192 N1 \u2192 N2 \u2192 N3 \u2192 N0) is a common, low-cost arrangement: each node only communicates with its two immediate neighbours, avoiding the switch complexity of all-to-all fabrics. Tensors move around this ring in a coordinated, cyclic pipeline rather than through random pairwise exchanges.\n\nAt the start of inference, the input batch is sharded or replicated depending on the parallelism strategy",
